## The idea

Depth means "how many levels". Breadth-first search handles **one whole level per loop**, so counting the loops gives the depth. It never recurses, so a 100,000-deep tree is fine. For the minimum depth, the same BFS **stops at the first leaf** it meets.

## Step by step

1. Empty tree → 0. Put the root in a queue.
2. While the queue is not empty: depth++, then take exactly `queue.Count` nodes (one level), queuing their children.
3. Return depth.
4. Minimum depth: same loop, but return depth as soon as a node has no children.

## Worked example

**Input:** tree [3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| level 1 | 3 → queue 9, 20 |
| level 2 | 9, 20 → queue 15, 7 |
| level 3 | 15, 7 → nothing queued |
| min depth | 9 is a leaf at level 2 → stop |

**Result:** max depth 3, min depth 2

## Cost

O(n) time; O(width of the tree) memory for the queue.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LinkedIn (reported Oct 2026, elimination round) — LeetCode 104 Maximum Depth of Binary Tree; follow-up LeetCode 111.
// TreeNode is provided by the starter (Models.cs): int Value; TreeNode Left, Right.

public static class TreeDepth {
    // Number of nodes on the longest root-to-leaf path. Level by level (BFS), so a very deep tree cannot overflow.
    public static int Max(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        var queue = new Queue<TreeNode>();
        queue.Enqueue(root);
        int depth = 0;
        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            depth++;                                    // one whole level per iteration
            int size = queue.Count;
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                var node = queue.Dequeue();
                if (node.Left != null) queue.Enqueue(node.Left);
                if (node.Right != null) queue.Enqueue(node.Right);
            }
        }
        return depth;
    }

    // Follow-up: nodes on the SHORTEST root-to-leaf path. BFS stops at the first leaf it meets.
    public static int Min(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        var queue = new Queue<TreeNode>();
        queue.Enqueue(root);
        int depth = 0;
        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            depth++;
            int size = queue.Count;
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                var node = queue.Dequeue();
                if (node.Left == null && node.Right == null) return depth;   // the nearest leaf
                if (node.Left != null) queue.Enqueue(node.Left);
                if (node.Right != null) queue.Enqueue(node.Right);
            }
        }
        return depth;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Depth in nodes or edges?**

Ask. LeetCode counts nodes; some interviewers count edges (one less).

**Q: Empty tree?**

0.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) time; O(width) memory for BFS, O(h) for DFS.

**Q: Recursive version?**

Three lines: 1 + max(left, right). Say it overflows on a deep chain, and offer BFS.

**Q: Shared state?**

Read-only tree.

**Q: Minimum depth on a huge tree.**

BFS wins: it stops at the first leaf; DFS must visit everything.

**Q: How would you test it?**

Empty, single node, lopsided, a deep chain, and a shallow leaf next to a deep branch.
